WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.952
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:11.360
DAN AHARON: Dialogflow is a tool
to help automate conversations.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.890
Typically, it can be used for
three main groups of use cases.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:19.250
It could be businesses that
want to talk to their customers

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.140
and helping automate
those conversations.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.400
It could be around customer
service or commerce.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.830
The second is powering
connected devices.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.240
So this could be connected
cars, connected TVs,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.450
and being able to intelligently
talk to the people that

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.410
want to use those devices.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.800
And the third is connecting
employees to their employers

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.710
and accessing information,
business intelligence.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.140
We're going to focus most
of today on the first set,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.560
but a lot of those tools
are going to be available

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.260
and are going to be useful
for the second and third group

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.600
of use cases, as well.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.260
So, you know, the space
is super exciting.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.040
We just chose, like,
four different statistics

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.540
to highlight here.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.000
There is, of course, much more.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.410
80% of customer interactions
could be resolved

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.640
with well-designed bots.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:19.400
That's mind boggling
if it actually

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.820
kind of sees itself out.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.740
My favorite are the
two on the bottom.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.380
So if you look at the bottom
left, a lot of businesses

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.550
today come to start using
bots to reduce costs

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.120
so they can serve users
and their customers

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:38.990
more efficiently.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.300
But, actually, in surveys,
the interesting thing

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.680
is customers actually
prefer self-serve tools when

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.490
they're asked what companies can
do to improve customer service.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:54.050
60% say that self-serve tools
is kind of the biggest thing

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.910
that they can do.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.700
And so there's, like, all
of these customers that

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.440
are kind of clamoring for
better self-serve tools,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.620
but they're forced
to be, you know,

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.660
talking to agents
for simple tasks

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:09.819
that don't really
justify agents.

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:10.610
And that last one--

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.260
50% of enterprises
will spend more

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.120
on bots than traditional
mobile development.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:21.290
That's also mind boggling if it
actually becomes true by 2021.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.730
So with all of that
excitement, the kind of problem

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.160
is, today, most of
the virtual agents

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.840
that are out there
are not very good.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.710
If you Google "why chat
bots," "what chat bots fail"

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.030
is the top result
that shows up, right?

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.770
It's pretty crazy.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:37.760
And why is that?

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.510
So here on the left
you can see an example.

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.090
There's all of these
bots that were built kind

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.795
of at the peak of the hype.

00:02:45.795 --> 00:02:47.420
A lot of companies
didn't really invest

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.040
in building them out properly.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.254
They're not very intelligent,
and it results in conversations

00:02:54.254 --> 00:02:54.920
that are broken.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.834
The good news is we
think, with Dialogflow,

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:01.250
there's a really
great opportunity

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.690
to build bots that
are intelligent.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.894
And so the way it
typically works

00:03:07.894 --> 00:03:10.310
in terms of architecture, what
we see customers deploying,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.500
there's all these
channels on the left.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:13.940
So it could be
text channels, chat

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.840
on the web, Facebook Messenger,
Google Assistant, or it

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.570
could be digital
voice in a mobile app,

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:25.430
in a car, in a TV, or phone
calls through a phone gateway.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.520
They come to Dialogflow,
which does the conversation

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.740
management and the natural
language understanding,

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.100
basically to break down, what
is the intent and the entities

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.050
in that natural language?

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.230
And then Dialogflow sends
that structured information

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:40.962
to the fulfillment layer.

00:03:40.962 --> 00:03:42.920
You know we think Google
Cloud is a great place

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.253
to host that fulfillment player.

00:03:44.253 --> 00:03:47.780
It can be on Cloud
Functions or Compute Engine.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.974
But you could also
host it anywhere else.

00:03:49.974 --> 00:03:51.890
You could put it on Prem
or wherever you want.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.330
How many people
here in the audience

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.250
were in the session about
Dialogflow last year

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.150
in Google Cloud Next?

00:04:01.831 --> 00:04:02.330
OK.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.730
A few of you.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.720
So at Google Cloud Next
last year, in our session,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.330
I announced that we
crossed 150,000 developers

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.400
for Dialogflow.

