00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.867
is it's just the quality of the
natural language, the quality

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:03.950
of the natural language
understanding, the machine

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:05.900
learning that
Dialogflow uses is head

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.420
and shoulders above anything
else that's out there,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.450
and it really makes
a difference, right?

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.090
Because what you
want to do is you

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.290
want to avoid things like that.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:16.790
Right?

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:18.830
And so it's much more
important than anything

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:22.860
else you could want
in a bot platform.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.090
So the exciting
thing is most of that

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:30.110
is due to the natural
language technology that

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.930
came from the Api.ai
acquisition that we did a year

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.050
and a half ago.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:38.450
And so what makes us
really, really excited

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:41.120
is that we're just
starting the journey

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.590
of making Dialogflow better.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.450
And now that it's
part of Google--

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.700
we, at Google, have actually
been solving this problem

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.158
with natural language
understanding

00:05:49.158 --> 00:05:51.920
for more than a decade in things
like Google Search, Google

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.022
Assistant, G-mail Smart
Reply, Translation.

00:05:55.022 --> 00:05:57.230
And we've built all of these
assets and capabilities,

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:00.620
like natural language
understanding engines, speech

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.810
recognition, TPUs.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.660
And all of these are now
available to Dialogflow

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.510
to build better tools.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.184
And so some of
these announcements

00:06:11.184 --> 00:06:13.100
you're going to hear
about today are basically

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:19.100
a result of this new Google
technology becoming available.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:26.820
So putting together this slide
kind of hammers the point.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.600
Just for me, personally,
I just realized how much

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.401
we've done in the last year.

00:06:31.401 --> 00:06:32.150
It's pretty crazy.

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:34.850
If you look at all of
the different launches

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:37.160
and feature enhancements
since our session

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.150
here last year at
Google Cloud Next '17,

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:42.740
we've added so much
to the product,

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:47.390
from built in analytics
to multi-language support,

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:53.045
inline code editor, Stackdriver
integration, versioning,

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.710
and lots more.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:07:04.100
And, today, I'm excited to
announce five new features that

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.700
make Dialogflow really well
suited to power the Enterprise

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:08.900
Contact Center.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.840
So what we've heard
from a lot of you

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.470
are there are a few things that
are still difficult to manage

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.080
in chat bot conversations.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.894
The first is connecting
bots to phones.

00:07:22.894 --> 00:07:25.310
So we're going to talk a little
bit more about that today.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.685
We're going to make it much
easier to connect the phones.

00:07:27.685 --> 00:07:33.320
Second is answering questions
that touch the long tail.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:35.600
Building Intents--
it's time consuming.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.150
Knowledge connectors are
here to help with that.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:39.920
Third is automatic
spell correction.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:43.310
So, as you guys know,
in chats, users often

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.550
make typos and mistakes.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:50.610
This uses Google
technology to solve that.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.970
Built-in sentiment analysis,
we are introducing today.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.890
That helps you
understand when you want

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:56.480
to transition to human agents.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.820
And last but not least,
built-in text-to-speech.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.560
That also powers
our phone gateway,

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.970
and it's built on
WaveNet technology.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.720
So I'll zoom
through these slides

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.129
pretty quickly so we
can go to the demo,

00:08:09.129 --> 00:08:11.420
and I'll show you guys how
some of these actually look.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:17.360
So our Google Phone
Gateway is built

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.330
on the same technology used
by Project Fi, Google Voice,

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:21.740
and Google Hangouts.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:25.820
It already powers over 20
million phone numbers today,

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.430
so it's already
operating at scale.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:31.190
Now it's available to
any Dialogflow user.

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:32.980
You can add a phone
number to your agent

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:36.140
in less than a minute.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.110
And the beauty of it-- it
wraps all of the technology

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:41.610
needed to make automated
phone calls happen,

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.179
including speech recognition,
natural language understanding,

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:46.430
speech synthesis, orchestration.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:47.930
It's all handled
by Google Cloud.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.580
Knowledge connectors-- we're
going to go deeper in the demo.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.740
So I'm going to skip that.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.840
Spelling correction--
so it looks pretty easy,

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.190
but it's really, really hard.

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:05.360
If we had to build something
that helps automate spelling

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.390
correction for bots
for Dialogflow,

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.340
it would have taken
us years and years

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.200
to create something
that's reasonably good,

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.620
and even then it's
hard to get it right.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.689
Luckily, because Dialogflow
is now part of Google,

00:09:19.689 --> 00:09:21.230
we were able to use
some of the tools

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:24.320
that Google uses for other
products like Search--

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:26.840
and I'm sure you guys have
all used spelling correction

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:28.470
in Search and in other places.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.590
And now Dialogflow
can take advantage

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:31.880
of those same capabilities.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.400
And this applies both to
Intents, like you can see here

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.160
on the right, and to Entities,
like you can see in the middle.

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:42.410
So built-in sentiment analysis--

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:45.890
a very common concern customers
have is, OK, I created a bot,

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:49.640
but how do I ensure there is
a great end user experience?

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.500
Built-in sentiment
analysis lets you

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.990
identify what the query
score is for every query

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.000
that the user
sends, and then you

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.530
can use that in
your business logic

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.613
to decide when you want
to switch to human agents.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:06.100
And last but not
least, text-to-speech

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:10.790
powered by DeepMind's
WaveNet gives you

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:15.470
the closest to human speech
that is possible for a bot,

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:17.360
and it's built in both
in our phone gateway

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:19.460
but also into Dialogflow
so you can use it

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.880
for IT and other uses.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.910
And the one other cool
thing that we just

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:31.460
announced this morning is we
also added device profiles.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:34.940
So what it does is we
actually shape the waveform

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:36.590
differently for our
speech synthesis

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:39.320
depending on which
speaker you use.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:41.270
So if you play it
on a phone line,

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:43.910
we'll produce speech
that's optimized for phone.

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:46.160
If you play it on
a large speaker,

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.030
we'll produce speech that's
suited for a large speaker.

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:51.860
OK.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:58.340
So let's try and see how this
all looks like in a demo.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:02.750
So those of you that were
in the session last year,

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:06.330
you'll remember what we did
was we built on stage here--

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.380
or, actually, in Moscone--

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:15.560
a bot that handles chats for
an imaginary Google hardware

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:16.430
store.

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:19.610
And it has an entity
called Products,

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:23.070
like Chromecast, Google Home,
Google Pixel, things like that.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:26.810
And then it has Intents, one
for service, one for commerce.

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:29.720
So if we look at the one
for commerce, for example,

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:33.500
it requires a product
address and quantity.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:42.740
And so if I say I'd
like to buy Chromecasts,

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:45.550
you can see it identifies
action as "buy."

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:47.050
The product is
Chromecast, but there

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.160
is no quantity and no address.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:51.170
And now it asks, what
address would you

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:52.700
like me to ship it to?

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:58.820
I can say 3 Third Street,
San Francisco, California.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.530
How many units do you want?

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.349
Five units.

00:12:02.349 --> 00:12:03.140
And then it's done.

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:06.110
It's ordering five Chromecasts,
shipping to 3 Third Street,

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:08.490
San Francisco, California.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.550
And there's a full JSON--

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:15.515
I think this window
is not showing up.

00:12:15.515 --> 00:12:18.510
OK, there it is.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.990
There is a full JSON you can
use in your fulfillment layer

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:23.400
to act on it.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:29.690
So what I'm going
to do now is, what

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:32.690
if you wanted to not just
help people buy stuff,

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:36.270
but also answer questions people
have about Google products?

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:44.630
So lets look for a
Chromecast FAQ web page.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:48.300
And we're just going to choose
the first result that's here.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:53.570
Let's take this URL and copy
it over, and let's add it

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:55.430
as a Knowledge Connector.

00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:57.740
So we need to create
a Knowledge Base.

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:04.970
Let's call it My Knowledge Base,
and let's create a Knowledge

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:06.470
Item.

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:10.490
We call it Store FAQ.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.100
This is going to be HTML.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.140
It's going to be an FAQ.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:17.120
And I'm going to
paste in the URL here.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:22.660
And what it's doing now,
it's going to this URL.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:27.100
It's downloading
this article or FAQ,

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.680
and it's going through the
questions and sorting them out.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:30.180
There.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:30.940
It just finished.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:37.220
So I'll show you guys
the detail in a second.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:39.520
But it basically
extracted the information.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:42.700
And then the next thing we
want to do is add a response.

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:46.930
So the default response
here is Knowledge Answer 1.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:48.520
What happens many
times with FAQs,

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:50.770
though, is the customer is
going to ask a question.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:53.650
You may not have the exact
same questions in the FAQ.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:55.051
You may have something similar.

00:13:55.051 --> 00:13:56.800
And there actually
might be more than one.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:59.210
There might be two or
three that are similar.

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:04.340
So what we'll do is
we'll copy it over.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.700
Let's choose Facebook
Messenger, for example.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.670
If a user is on
Facebook Messenger,

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:13.360
they have the ability to
see more than one option.

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:19.810
So what we'll do is we'll
add cards in a carousel here

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:24.280
that show two different answers.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:29.500
And we'll use question
1 here, and, here,

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.879
it will be question
2 and answer 2.

00:14:31.879 --> 00:14:34.420
So we're going to show them both
the question we matched them

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:39.820
with, as well as the answer
so they know what we wanted.

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:40.360
OK.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.010
I'm going to click Save.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:48.220
And now, just so you
guys see the full detail,

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:49.884
you can see this
is what we scanned

00:14:49.884 --> 00:14:52.300
from that web site, all the
questions and all the answers.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:54.591
They're all here, and you
can even disable some of them

00:14:54.591 --> 00:14:57.920
if you don't want to
cover them in your bot.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:59.260
OK.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:00.670
So let's go back.

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.360
We'll try it out in a minute.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.960
But one other thing
I wanted to show you

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:09.730
guys is our new Phone Gateway.

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:11.800
So you can see this is
a new square we just

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.340
added this morning, the
Dialogflow Phone Gateway.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:17.710
And what it does, it lets
you add a new phone line

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:20.000
to your agent in seconds.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:21.110
So all I need to do--

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:23.860
I click the Dialogflow
Phone Gateway,

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:26.020
and now I choose an
area code if I want to.

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.960
I could also leave this
blank, but let's say

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:31.060
I want to choose Ohio.

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:33.550
I click Next.

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:35.370
Now I choose one
of these numbers.

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:37.410
I click Create.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:39.480
And that's done.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:41.790
So we now have a phone
number in this agent,

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:44.970
and it always starts
from the Welcome Intent.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:49.090
So we'll go to the Welcome
Intent in a second.

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.310
Let's first test our
knowledge connectors.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:16:02.310
So if, for example, let's
say there's a question here,

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:06.930
how is this different
from Apple's AirPlay?

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:10.690
So let's try this
question in Dialogflow,

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:12.440
and let's see if it works.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:18.070
It needs a minute to update.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:28.980
OK.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:29.920
There it is.

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:34.400
So you can see it
gives the same--

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:40.520
it retrieves this whole
answer from the article,

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:44.160
even though the words
I use are a little

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:48.090
different from the article.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:50.090
So I didn't use the same
words for the question.

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:51.870
It knows how to match it.

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:54.180
Now, it looks very easy,
and for humans, this

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:56.340
is a very easy task.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:57.810
Those of you in
the audience that

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.185
have done a little bit of
natural language understanding,

00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:03.690
they know it's actually
pretty hard for a computer

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:05.490
to understand how
to match things

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:07.770
like that in natural
language, because things

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:12.050
sound very similar when you
look at it programatically.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:20.339
So let's look at it in Facebook
Messenger, and let's try--

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:22.666
now there's two
different questions

00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:23.790
here about remote controls.

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:25.248
Do I need a separate
remote control

00:17:25.248 --> 00:17:26.580
for volume and other things?

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:28.830
Can you use Chromecast with
a physical remote control?

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:37.260
If I ask, how do
remote controls work,

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:40.720
it could be matched to either
one of those questions,

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:43.490
so it needs to update.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:52.970
OK.

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:58.160
So you can see it matches
to both of those questions,

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.040
so it detects that
both of them work.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.890
Now, this is really, really
hard, because, remember,

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:06.500
this is the same agent we're
using that also has the bot

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:07.410
technology.

00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:17.570
So if I say I'd like
to buy a Chromecast,

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:20.360
it needs to both handle
the commerce actions

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:22.070
that we gave it, as
well as the FAQs,

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:25.460
and know when to
use each of them.

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:28.460
So it's non-trivial,
and there's a lot

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:32.450
of natural language work that's
happening behind the scenes.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:37.430
So let's see it all coming
together in our phone demo.

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:41.160
So this is the phone number.

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.040
I'm just going to try it out.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:46.160
Now, speech recognition
demos are always risky,

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:47.180
so bear with me here.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:50.680
Hopefully, this
is going to work.

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:57.530
I'm going to try it on speaker
phone, which is not the best,

00:18:57.530 --> 00:18:58.730
but let's see.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:18:59.300
OK.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:01.610
So if this works, what
it should be doing--

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.830
it should be going
to our Welcome Intent

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:07.590
and then reading what we
have in the Welcome Intent.

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:08.289
Let's try.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:15.140
PHONE BOT 1: --to the
Dialogflow phone demo.

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:16.730
How can I help you today?

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:19.430
You can order new Google
products or request service

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:22.760
on existing ones.

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.980
DAN AHARON: What's the
difference between Chromecast

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:26.030
and Apple AirPlay?

00:19:29.162 --> 00:19:30.620
PHONE BOT 1: In
most cases, AirPlay

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:33.110
works by beaming the
video or audio directly

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:36.680
from an iOS device to
the television over WiFi.

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:38.055
Your iPhone or
iPad must remain--

00:19:38.055 --> 00:19:39.971
DAN AHARON: So everything
you're hearing right

00:19:39.971 --> 00:19:41.940
now is not typed into
Dialogflow or anything.

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:45.020
It was ingested automatically
from a random article

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:46.550
that someone wrote
on the internet.

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:47.925
PHONE BOT 1: --to
the television,

00:19:47.925 --> 00:19:51.100
which then starts streaming
straight from the internet.

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:53.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:53.950
DAN AHARON: Thank you.

00:19:57.275 --> 00:19:59.682
PHONE BOT 1: --for beaming
the actual content.

00:19:59.682 --> 00:20:01.640
If there's an advantage
to AirPlay, it's that--

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.622
DAN AHARON: Let's see if the
regular Intents still work.

00:20:05.622 --> 00:20:07.450
PHONE BOT 1: --but
even this is becoming

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:09.841
less of a differentiator.

00:20:09.841 --> 00:20:11.132
DAN AHARON: It's a long answer.

00:20:11.132 --> 00:20:11.631
Yeah.

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:18.500
Can I buy a few Google Pixels?

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:21.880
PHONE BOT 1: What address would
you like me to ship it to?

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:25.850
DAN AHARON: 3 Seventh
Street, New York, New York.

00:20:25.850 --> 00:20:28.170
PHONE BOT 1: How many
units do you want?

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:28.920
DAN AHARON: Seven.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.790
PHONE BOT 1: Ordering seven
Google Pixel and shipping to 3

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:37.400
Seventh Street,
New York, New York.

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:39.766
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:46.960
DAN AHARON: So I just want
to emphasize all of that

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:49.000
looked really simple
to set up, and we

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:51.610
were able to do it in
less than a minute.

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:56.320
Some of those things, you're
able to do with other services,

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:58.780
but other services that are
out there in the market today

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:02.140
require you to set up a
phone gateway, and that's

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:04.660
something that will take you
probably a few hours to set up

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:06.430
with everything involved,
and who knows how much you

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:07.221
need to pay for it.

00:21:07.221 --> 00:21:11.050
And then you need to connect
it to sort of the relevant bot

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:12.070
service.

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:16.360
And then if you want to
set up a knowledge service,

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:19.930
you need to set
that up separately,

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:23.110
and then you need to figure out
how many kind of gigabytes you

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:25.867
need for it, because it's
usually server based instead

00:21:25.867 --> 00:21:26.700
of being serverless.

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:30.340
And then you need to connect
all of these components

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:31.330
and do a lot of coding.

00:21:31.330 --> 00:21:33.830
We basically did all of
that with zero coding

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:35.620
in less than a
minute, and you have

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:38.480
kind of a full automated
bot that works on the phone.

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:41.710
So we're very excited
with this, and we

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:45.180
can't wait to see what
you guys all do with it.

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:45.680
Thank you.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:47.528
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:53.609
So with that, let
me invite Tariq.

00:21:53.609 --> 00:21:54.900
TARIQ EL-KHATIB: Hi, everybody.

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:55.850
I'm Tariq El-Khatib.

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:58.310
I'm the product
manager at Ticketmaster

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:01.010
within the Global Contact Center
and Technology Department.

00:22:01.010 --> 00:22:03.980
If you don't already know,
Ticketmaster sells tickets.

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:05.990
A lot of tickets.

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:09.920
In 2017, Ticketmaster
sold 292 million tickets.

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:12.020
2% of those were
over the telephone

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:13.580
and through our contact centers.

00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:16.310
So it's safe to say we receive
several million calls per year.

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:28.610
Ticketmaster is a division
of Live Nation Entertainment.

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:31.370
Because of this, we
receive a large number

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:34.320
of calls of all sorts
of types of events.

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.290
So on top of our
common questions

00:22:36.290 --> 00:22:39.890
or ticket questions, such
as, I lost my tickets,

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:43.550
or how do I print my tickets,
we receive a lot of questions

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:47.960
that we categorize as general
questions, and many of those

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:51.200
are event and venue
specific, such as,

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:54.290
where do I find the will call
box office at Wrigley Field,

00:22:54.290 --> 00:22:59.060
or how big is the camping
area at Paradiso Festival?

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:00.652
Before I jump in
and show you how

00:23:00.652 --> 00:23:02.360
we're using Dialogflow
and Knowledge Base

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:06.560
Connectors integrated into
our customer service IVR,

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:09.020
I wanted to go
through and show you

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:11.600
two slides that really
highlight the impact

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:16.430
that an NLP like Dialogflow
could make on to our sales IVR.

00:23:16.430 --> 00:23:19.700
So over here is the transcript
of Dan calling the Ticketmaster

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:20.390
sales IVR.

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:24.780
It's a speech-enabled IVR
that was built 10 years ago.

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:27.770
At that time, it
was top of the line.

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:31.460
But the technology then
required short phrases

00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:36.050
and didn't have any intent
or entity extraction.

00:23:36.050 --> 00:23:38.990
So between Dan talking and
the system understanding,

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:41.540
it takes 10 attempts for--

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:44.312
and one missed
recognition-- for the system

00:23:44.312 --> 00:23:45.770
to understand that
Dan wants to buy

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:48.410
two tickets to see the
Chainsmokers in San

00:23:48.410 --> 00:23:50.730
Francisco on May 5.

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:53.820
Now, seeing how that
would look like with a NLP

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:57.270
like Dialogflow, this
can be completed in one

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:59.340
to two transactions.

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:03.090
This is obviously quite an
upgrade in customer experience

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.370
or user experience,
but the system also

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:09.260
has the flexibility to walk the
customer through each prompt

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:12.280
if they still prefer to do so.

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:12.780
All right.

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:15.810
Now I'm going to go ahead
and jump into our integration

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:17.557
with our customer service IVR.

00:24:17.557 --> 00:24:19.140
I'm going to be
showing you two calls.

00:24:19.140 --> 00:24:23.460
This first call is what I would
categorize as a common ticket

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:24.854
type call.

00:24:24.854 --> 00:24:26.520
PHONE SPEAKER 1: A
couple months back, I

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:28.972
had bought tickets to
the Yankees game, which

00:24:28.972 --> 00:24:30.180
is happening now in two days.

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:33.644
I still haven't received
my actual tickets.

00:24:33.644 --> 00:24:35.810
TARIQ EL-KHATIB: So the
caller is calling and saying

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:38.030
they haven't received
their Yankees tickets.

00:24:38.030 --> 00:24:42.541
This recording is captured, then
coded and sent into our Intent

00:24:42.541 --> 00:24:43.040
Service.

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:46.790
Our Intent Service houses a lot
of our complex business rules.

00:24:46.790 --> 00:24:49.130
That then connects to
our external proxy,

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:51.260
which acts as a traffic
router between all

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:55.670
of our external services and
also translates all of that API

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:57.890
traffic into a generic
API to feed back

00:24:57.890 --> 00:24:59.480
into the Intent Service.

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:01.595
So once the call
reaches external proxy,

00:25:01.595 --> 00:25:04.700
it's then sent to
Dialogflow right here

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:09.710
on the right, Agent 1
that is our main NLP,

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:12.620
and that triggers an intent
of tickets not received

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:16.250
with the entity of
sports team, the Yankees.

00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:19.040
That information is then sent
back through the pipeline

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:21.489
to the external proxy, then
back to the Intent Service,

00:25:21.489 --> 00:25:23.030
where, in the Intent
Service, there's

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:27.610
a rule that's triggered
based off of that intent that

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:29.810
plays a landmark
prompt, let me see if I

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:31.580
can help you find your tickets.

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:33.800
And then based off of
the entity of the Yankees

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:35.930
and the customer's
incoming phone number,

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:38.510
we're able to tell that this
customer had a mobile ticket

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:40.850
order and inform them,
looks like your tickets

00:25:40.850 --> 00:25:45.430
are available right now within
your Ticketmaster mobile app.

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:50.420
So now I'm going to go
ahead and show a more event

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:52.574
specific question.

00:25:52.574 --> 00:25:54.240
PHONE SPEAKER 2: I
was calling to just--

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:55.615
I have a general question.

00:25:55.615 --> 00:25:59.360
I'm trying to get an idea of how
big these camping spaces would

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:03.959
be, just the general size
at the Paradiso Festival.

00:26:03.959 --> 00:26:05.500
TARIQ EL-KHATIB: So
the question was,

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:07.420
how big is the
area of the camping

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:09.490
space at Paradiso Festival?

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:11.440
Just like call
one, the recording

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:13.280
is captured and
coded and then sent

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:15.670
to our Intent Service
and then external proxy.

00:26:15.670 --> 00:26:19.180
And then it hits that same
Agent 1, our main NLP.

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:23.860
That's where it triggers
a intent that we labeled

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:26.390
as event and venue
specific question,

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:30.635
and it also yields the
entity of Paradiso Festival.

00:26:30.635 --> 00:26:32.260
So that information
is then routed back

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:34.990
through the pipeline, where it
triggers a rule on the Intent

00:26:34.990 --> 00:26:38.400
Service to tell the
external proxy to reprocess

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:43.630
the same transcribed
text from the first call,

00:26:43.630 --> 00:26:46.630
but this time, based off
of the entity of Paradiso,

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:52.450
to hit an agent that is event
specific with the Knowledge

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:56.680
Base Connector tied to the
Paradiso Festival FAQs.

00:26:56.680 --> 00:27:01.060
So, there, the answer's then
retrieved from the FAQ site,

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:02.860
goes through back the
pipeline, and that's

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:05.590
where the customer
will hear the camping

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:08.530
area at Paradiso Festival
is 25 by 15 feet,

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:12.530
enough to fit one
car and one tent.

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:17.530
So I'm going to go ahead
and show you how this looks

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:20.290
like in Swagger, the back end.

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:22.970
So, right here, I've got
the encoded recording,

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:25.460
and go ahead and
click Try It Out.

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:28.090
And so what's going on now
is it's making that full trip

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:31.470
through the Intent Service to
external proxy, Dialogflow,

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:32.830
and back.

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:36.550
And then here's the result.
As we see, there's no error.

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:38.140
That's good.

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:42.040
The actual transcript
of the call--

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:44.920
the caller just had
a general question.

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:47.170
And then the intent is
correctly identified

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:50.710
as event venue specific
with the festival

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:52.290
name of Paradiso Festival.

00:27:54.820 --> 00:28:00.430
So also, below here, you'll
also see the additional data

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:02.380
that we appended,
additional business logic,

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:04.660
like landmark and
confirmation prompt,

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:08.020
or if there's a specific action
that needs to happen based off

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:10.150
of this intent type.

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:14.862
So the second leg of the call
where it actually goes and hits

00:28:14.862 --> 00:28:16.570
the Knowledge Base
Connector is something

00:28:16.570 --> 00:28:18.250
we are currently developing.

00:28:18.250 --> 00:28:19.730
So for the purposes
of this demo,

00:28:19.730 --> 00:28:22.720
I'm going to go ahead and
act as a courier pigeon

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:24.670
and show what that looks like.

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:40.690
So you could see
the question is now

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:44.635
returned as a general standing
camping site is about 15

00:28:44.635 --> 00:28:47.980
by 25 feet, enough for
a tent and vehicle.

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:51.250
And so it can actually answer
these event specific and venue

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:54.100
questions now in our
self-service IVR,

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:57.920
which previously would
need to route to an agent.

00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:04.580
So to wrap things up, here are
our key takeaways and lessons

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:05.450
learned.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:07.667
With a wide spectrum
of potential intents,

00:29:07.667 --> 00:29:10.250
you really should consider using
multiple agents and knowledge

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:10.750
bases.

00:29:10.750 --> 00:29:14.210
This can really help with
intents that are very related

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:16.520
but are still
somewhat different.

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:18.500
Also, consider creating
an external proxy

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:22.310
with a generic API for product
and feature scalability.

00:29:22.310 --> 00:29:24.500
So, when we built
this external proxy,

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:28.220
part of my future roadmap was
to also include a sentiment

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:31.250
analyzer, but that was
before Google would

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:32.570
include that in Dialogflow.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:35.660
So I at least have
the ability to now--

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:37.730
any other additional
advances in AI--

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:40.920
to easily integrate
it with our system.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.150
Next is conversation matters.

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:44.710
Put yourself in
the user's shoes,

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:46.870
and anticipate what
the next question is.

00:29:46.870 --> 00:29:50.900
And, especially with IVRs,
gotta break the old, rigid IVR

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:52.340
prompting habits.

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:58.414
Those are not conversational, so
these services are only as good

00:29:58.414 --> 00:29:59.830
as the effort and
the conversation

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:02.140
that you actually put into them.

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:04.480
Finally, think big with AI.

00:30:04.480 --> 00:30:07.660
Every day, we see new
advances in the AI space.

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:10.870
For example, looking at the
phone connectors that were just

00:30:10.870 --> 00:30:12.700
announced today,
in theory, we could

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:16.300
utilize those to create a
unique IVR for every major event

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:19.960
that we have or even
every major venue.

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:26.320
So based on your
everyday business,

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:29.350
you should really be
evaluating how these new AI

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:32.027
advances really can make a
difference to your business.

00:30:32.027 --> 00:30:33.610
So with that, I'm
going to hand it off

00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:35.320
to Akash for Marks and Spencers.

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:37.312
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:43.230 --> 00:30:44.480
AKASH PARMAR: Hello, everyone.

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:45.680
I'm Akash Parmar.

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:48.520
I'm the enterprise architect
at Marks and Spencer.

00:30:48.520 --> 00:30:51.550
Anyone here who's never
heard of Marks and Spencer?

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:52.120
Excellent.

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:54.130
That slide for you.

00:30:54.130 --> 00:30:59.500
Established in 1884,
we have 1,500 stores,

00:30:59.500 --> 00:31:02.770
and we have 81,000 employees,
32 million customers

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:04.660
across the world.

00:31:04.660 --> 00:31:07.030
It's one of the most
recognized brands,

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:10.270
and it's a very popular
British household name.

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:14.440
And just to emphasize how
important Marks and Spencer is

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:17.680
to its customers, one
in three women in the UK

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:22.630
buy their bras from M&amp;S, and we
sell 45 of them every minute.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:24.460
In terms of our
contact center, we

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:27.370
take about 15 million
interactions over voice, chat,

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:30.640
and email, and that's handled
by [? 1,500 ?] advisors

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:34.350
and by our store staff, as well.

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:37.560
So our leadership team
gave us a challenge

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:39.960
where we were
asked to understand

00:31:39.960 --> 00:31:41.640
our customers better.

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:44.580
We were asked to provide
more self-service options,

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:48.110
and we were also
asked to save cost.

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:51.020
And to achieve that, the
first thing we had to do

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:55.070
was to get rid of
our rigid DTMF IVRs,

00:31:55.070 --> 00:31:57.800
and we replaced that with
a natural language based

00:31:57.800 --> 00:31:59.780
solution, which
would ask a very open

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:03.610
ended question like,
how may we help you?

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:06.170
The response from
the customers was

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:08.810
digested into Dialogflow,
which would then come back

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:11.240
with an actionable intent.

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:15.110
This solution-- it's
not a deep solution

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:17.810
going into the depths
of Dialogflow, which is

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:19.530
our aspiration in the future.

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:23.120
But it is a solution which
was implemented widely

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:24.980
across our organization.

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:29.120
And this platform would
take over 12 million calls

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:31.030
over the next 12 months.

00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:35.020
What this did was it freed
up around 100 employees, who

00:32:35.020 --> 00:32:38.620
were just busy taking calls
and moving the calls around,

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:42.160
to actually not handle those
calls and be on the shop floor

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:45.790
and actually work with the
customers who are walking

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:47.930
in and out of our shops.

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:50.450
As you can see on
the side, my boss

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:53.540
calls it the stairway to
our customer service heaven.

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:56.330
We are still on step two,
so we have a long way to go.

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:02.010
Quick view of our current
solution-- what I'll do

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:06.360
is I'll try and make a call just
to give an experience of what

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:07.140
it sounds like.

00:33:15.521 --> 00:33:17.720
PHONE BOT 2:
Welcome to M&amp;S. Just

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:20.175
so you know, we record our
calls to help with training.

00:33:20.175 --> 00:33:23.920
So that we can get you to
the best person to help,

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:26.290
please, can you tell
us in a few words

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:27.615
why you are calling today?

00:33:27.615 --> 00:33:29.490
AKASH PARMAR: I want to
buy some blue shirts.

00:33:33.282 --> 00:33:34.719
[PHONE RINGING]

00:33:34.719 --> 00:33:36.260
PHONE BOT 2: We'd
really like to know

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:38.345
what you think of the
service you received today.

00:33:38.345 --> 00:33:39.470
AKASH PARMAR: So the call--

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:40.820
just going to the flow, then.

00:33:40.820 --> 00:33:43.610
What happened was,
I made a call.

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:45.770
It went into the
Twilio platform, which

00:33:45.770 --> 00:33:49.790
answered the call, and it then
invoked our application, which

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:52.160
is in our secure environment.

00:33:52.160 --> 00:33:55.160
The application said,
how may we help you?

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:57.840
The customer said, I want
to buy some blue shirts.

00:33:57.840 --> 00:34:01.550
It then used Google's Speech
API to convert the speech

00:34:01.550 --> 00:34:02.690
into text.

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:06.290
That text was handed back to our
application, which then sent it

00:34:06.290 --> 00:34:07.672
across to Dialogflow.

00:34:07.672 --> 00:34:09.949
Dialogflow came
back with an intent,

00:34:09.949 --> 00:34:12.940
and we then use that intent to
route the call to our contact

00:34:12.940 --> 00:34:14.530
center.

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:17.679
So that's how it
looks like today.

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:21.550
The partnership between
Twilio and Dialogflow

00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:23.560
is going to make
this much simpler.

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:26.739
I wish they had this partnership
six to eight months ago.

00:34:26.739 --> 00:34:30.130
It would make my life much
easier, but it's now here,

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:33.040
and I think it is a very,
very great opportunity.

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:37.033
So what happens now is Twilio
would integrate directly

00:34:37.033 --> 00:34:37.699
with Dialogflow.

00:34:37.699 --> 00:34:42.624
So rather than just coming back
with the utterance or the text,

00:34:42.624 --> 00:34:45.310
it will actually come
back with an intent.

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:49.929
And then the organizations
can act on that intent very,

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:50.510
very quickly.

00:34:50.510 --> 00:34:53.620
So it will, A,
make things faster,

00:34:53.620 --> 00:34:56.960
and B, it will help
experiment quicker, as well.

00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:01.840
So it's a very, very exciting
opportunity, this partnership

00:35:01.840 --> 00:35:03.330
between Dialogflow and Twilio.

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:08.980
This is our current
dashboard, how it looks like.

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:13.710
Let me see if I can flip
across to the live dashboard.

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:17.760
And, hopefully, we'll find
the call which I just made.

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:33.210
So, basically, the dashboard--

00:35:33.210 --> 00:35:37.380
it's a quick view on how
many calls we are taking,

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:42.160
what are our top 10 intents,
what the date is today.

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:43.740
Oh, my blue shirts,
they are there.

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:46.187
So the top call--

00:35:46.187 --> 00:35:48.270
that's my call, so I want
to buy some blue shirts.

00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:51.080
So very clearly, you
can see, calls come in.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:53.670
The utterance was transcribed
very, very accurately

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:55.504
by Google Speech API.

00:35:55.504 --> 00:35:56.295
Went to Dialogflow.

00:35:56.295 --> 00:35:59.400
Dialogflow said it's an order
call, and then based on that,

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:01.510
we said we're going to
send it to the sales team

00:36:01.510 --> 00:36:02.843
so that they can take the order.

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:14.100
Two key things
which I'll take away

00:36:14.100 --> 00:36:16.260
or which we have achieved--

00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:20.250
A, tool like Dialogflow
was so simple to be

00:36:20.250 --> 00:36:21.730
used by the business users.

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:23.910
So from day one,
they were the one

00:36:23.910 --> 00:36:28.430
who built it, trained the agent,
and they did it within days,

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:33.890
and we achieve now 90%
accuracy on our intent.

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:36.320
The second benefit,
which we didn't even

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:40.780
realize we'll get at the
start of this journey,

00:36:40.780 --> 00:36:45.260
was the fact that we could now
ask the advisors not to fill

00:36:45.260 --> 00:36:46.875
in any reason for contact.

00:36:46.875 --> 00:36:50.690
Rather than the
advisor telling us

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:52.880
what they think the
call was all about,

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:56.090
we actually let the
customer intent do that.

00:36:56.090 --> 00:36:59.225
So it's what the customer
wants, and they have said it

00:36:59.225 --> 00:37:02.210
in their own words, and we
then use that as our reason

00:37:02.210 --> 00:37:02.780
for contact.

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:08.150
And it's saved, like, 10 seconds
from every call in [? AHT. ?]

00:37:08.150 --> 00:37:10.350
So we have some big
plans with Dialogflow,

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:13.710
so now we're going to
start going deeper.

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:15.350
First thing we want
to try to achieve

00:37:15.350 --> 00:37:18.800
is an end-to-end conversational
order fulfillment journey.

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:22.160
So identify the customers,
integrate to the back end,

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:23.750
and also take payments.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:28.620
So that's now a big
aspiration of ours.

00:37:28.620 --> 00:37:31.620
We also want to use it for any
kind of appointment booking

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:32.120
system.

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:34.640
So we get a lot of
calls around bra fitting

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:36.600
appointments in our stores.

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:38.180
So we want to automate that.

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:42.530
We want to also go out and
use home devices, where

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:45.950
we have a dine in for 10
pounds offer in M&amp;S, which

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:47.000
is very, very popular.

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:50.570
So, Google Home, Alexa can
be used by the customers

00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:52.710
to find out whether we
have an offer ongoing.

00:37:52.710 --> 00:37:54.980
If yes, what's this all about?

00:37:54.980 --> 00:37:57.800
And also some complex journeys
like flower ordering, which

00:37:57.800 --> 00:38:00.501
is, again, a very
big business for M&amp;S,

00:38:00.501 --> 00:38:02.000
but a complex
journey, because there

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:05.870
could be many reasons for which
you'll be ordering flowers--

00:38:05.870 --> 00:38:08.510
happy, sad-- so
sentiments get involved.

00:38:08.510 --> 00:38:13.880
So that's a journey we
want to go after, as well.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:15.840
Key challenges--
so one challenge

00:38:15.840 --> 00:38:19.400
we had was we had to integrate
Dialogflow separately,

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:21.720
and now that would go
away with the partnership

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:24.600
between Dialogflow and Twilio.

00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:28.350
So that's a step in
the right direction.

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:30.930
Experiment rapidly,
but remember,

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:32.880
there is no prize if
you can't productionize.

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:37.190
It's all great to have all
these tools and technology,

00:38:37.190 --> 00:38:39.810
but then if we can't
productionize them and send

00:38:39.810 --> 00:38:43.900
customer calls through
it, it's of no use.

00:38:43.900 --> 00:38:48.180
We need to also be aware of our
internal governance challenges.

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:50.160
So it took us, like,
five weeks to get

00:38:50.160 --> 00:38:53.160
our first [? MVP, ?] but it took
us five months to productionize

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:53.965
it.

00:38:53.965 --> 00:38:56.100
So procurement,
legal, finance, you

00:38:56.100 --> 00:38:59.430
need to make sure you
take them on the journey

00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:00.640
from the very start.

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:04.000
Otherwise, you would be
hitting lots of blockers.

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:06.050
To have a lean team
with the right attitude.

00:39:06.050 --> 00:39:09.940
So I think my boss, Chris
McGraw, who's in the audience,

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:12.520
takes the phrase "learn
from your mistakes"

00:39:12.520 --> 00:39:14.109
very, very seriously,
and he gets

00:39:14.109 --> 00:39:16.150
upset if you don't make
five mistakes every week.

00:39:16.150 --> 00:39:17.600
So that's what we do.

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:19.690
We make mistakes, and we
learn from our mistakes,

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:22.660
and it has worked for us so far.

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:24.770
Get the right
technology partners.

00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:28.590
We had that in
Twilio and in Google.

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:32.500
Twilio was at every step with us
in our initial implementation.

00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:37.190
It helped us recognize the
potential of this platform.

00:39:37.190 --> 00:39:40.030
And we thank them for that.

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:44.590
The accuracy of Google Speech
API, the accuracy of Dialogflow

00:39:44.590 --> 00:39:47.284
gave us confidence to go back
to the business and tell them,

00:39:47.284 --> 00:39:49.450
this is something which is
definitely going to work.

00:39:49.450 --> 00:39:53.050
It's not a question.

00:39:53.050 --> 00:39:55.150
We know it's going to
work, and we went there

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:58.990
with total confidence.

00:39:58.990 --> 00:40:01.724
Partners where we don't
have internal resources.

00:40:01.724 --> 00:40:03.140
We didn't have a
development team,

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:04.529
but we wanted to move fast.

00:40:04.529 --> 00:40:06.070
So we partnered with
a company called

00:40:06.070 --> 00:40:09.560
DVELP, whose CEO, Tom Mullen,
is in the audience, as well.

00:40:09.560 --> 00:40:11.770
So the key message
there is make sure you

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:14.150
have the right team
behind you when

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:16.000
you set off on this journey.

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:21.040
And, also, the technology
landscape is changing so fast.

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:24.550
You have to make sure that
it's your architecture--

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:27.340
your applications
are open to that.

00:40:27.340 --> 00:40:29.000
New things are
coming in every day.

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:32.360
And we have to adapt to it
very, very quickly, as well.

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:35.470
So just to conclude, can
Dialogflow virtual agents

00:40:35.470 --> 00:40:36.720
help contact centers?

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:39.430
We at Marks and Spencer are
definitely in the yes camp.

00:40:39.430 --> 00:40:40.240
Thank you.

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:41.553
I'll pass it on to Jared now.

00:40:41.553 --> 00:40:43.386
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:43.386 --> 00:40:44.511
JARED MOORE: Thanks, Akash.

