How many of you have been
to a Home Depot before?

00:40:55.700 --> 00:40:56.200
Awesome.

00:40:56.200 --> 00:40:56.897
Yes.

00:40:56.897 --> 00:40:59.230
So in case you weren't aware,
we are the world's largest

00:40:59.230 --> 00:41:00.710
home improvement retailer.

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:04.540
We have over 400,000 associates
and over 2,000 stores

00:41:04.540 --> 00:41:07.249
across the United States,
Canada, and Mexico.

00:41:07.249 --> 00:41:09.040
And we also have one
of the world's largest

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:10.540
e-commerce websites.

00:41:10.540 --> 00:41:12.012
So today what I'm going to do--

00:41:12.012 --> 00:41:13.470
by the way, my name
is Jared Moore.

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:15.511
I'm from the Voice and
Conversational Search Team

00:41:15.511 --> 00:41:16.100
at Home Depot.

00:41:16.100 --> 00:41:18.100
And we're going to show
you a new feature that's

00:41:18.100 --> 00:41:21.560
available in our Home Depot app.

00:41:21.560 --> 00:41:24.310
So now what I'm showing
you here is our beta app.

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:26.980
And I actually just got word
that this feature is live on

00:41:26.980 --> 00:41:28.750
or Android app, and
it's going to be live

00:41:28.750 --> 00:41:30.371
pretty soon on an iOS app.

00:41:30.371 --> 00:41:31.870
So if you all don't
have it already,

00:41:31.870 --> 00:41:34.000
go ahead, download the
latest update of our app,

00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:36.561
and give it a try
after the talk.

00:41:36.561 --> 00:41:38.810
So what we developed is a
new version of voice search.

00:41:38.810 --> 00:41:42.740
So now you can go and hit
the microphone button.

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:43.990
I'm looking for a hammer.

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:49.030
And it still worked.

00:41:49.030 --> 00:41:53.110
So, you see, Dialogflow was able
to parse out that I'm actually

00:41:53.110 --> 00:41:54.110
looking for is a hammer.

00:41:54.110 --> 00:41:56.776
We don't actually want to search
for "I'm looking for a hammer,"

00:41:56.776 --> 00:41:59.590
and then go and put that
into the search box for us.

00:41:59.590 --> 00:42:01.090
And, in the future,
we're also going

00:42:01.090 --> 00:42:06.130
to use Dialogflow to have
an audio response confirming

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:07.990
to the user that what
we're searching for

00:42:07.990 --> 00:42:09.615
is actually what they
want to look for.

00:42:13.360 --> 00:42:13.860
Cool.

00:42:13.860 --> 00:42:14.850
So how did we do it?

00:42:14.850 --> 00:42:18.169
So we designed a five layer
architecture to enable this,

00:42:18.169 --> 00:42:19.710
and the first layer
is pretty simple.

00:42:19.710 --> 00:42:22.209
It's just a client, so anything
that a customer or associate

00:42:22.209 --> 00:42:23.540
is going to talk to.

00:42:23.540 --> 00:42:25.910
The clients then connect
to a proxy layer.

00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:27.590
So if we want to have
an optional layer

00:42:27.590 --> 00:42:30.380
to do authentication,
then we add it there.

00:42:30.380 --> 00:42:32.750
And then the real
intelligence is

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:34.360
when we go to the intent layer.

00:42:34.360 --> 00:42:37.250
This is Dialogflow, and it
provides the automatic speech

00:42:37.250 --> 00:42:40.190
recognition, as you saw,
intent matching, as well

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:42.570
as, in the future, TTS.

00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:44.990
Dialogflow then goes
to our routing layer.

00:42:44.990 --> 00:42:48.410
And what we noticed
was, if I say

00:42:48.410 --> 00:42:51.710
I'm looking for a hammer
to my mobile phone,

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:53.711
on the desktop site,
on a Google Home,

00:42:53.711 --> 00:42:55.460
no matter what, I'm
looking for something.

00:42:55.460 --> 00:42:56.540
And the way that
you respond to me

00:42:56.540 --> 00:42:58.399
might be different
depending on what channel

00:42:58.399 --> 00:42:59.190
I'm talking to you.

00:42:59.190 --> 00:43:01.320
But no matter what,
it's the same intent.

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:04.500
So what we do is we route
everything to a routing layer

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:07.730
and then route based
on where the client is.

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:09.620
So if you were talking
to the desktop site,

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:11.078
we'll get the
response specifically

00:43:11.078 --> 00:43:12.420
for the desktop back end.

00:43:12.420 --> 00:43:12.920
All right.

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:16.766
So in our example, we showed
you the page for hammers,

00:43:16.766 --> 00:43:18.140
but if you were
on a Google Home,

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:19.890
then we couldn't
actually show you a page.

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:22.100
So we'd have a different
response for that case,

00:43:22.100 --> 00:43:24.160
and we want to be able
to handle that here.

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:24.660
All right.

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:28.040
So if I had to narrow this
down into three main things

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:30.870
that you should focus on,
first of all, like I just said,

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:32.750
separate your
actions from intents.

00:43:32.750 --> 00:43:36.080
You're really going to see that
intents are pretty much going

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:39.270
to be the same, no matter
where you're talking to.

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:41.010
If you want to
find something, you

00:43:41.010 --> 00:43:42.500
are going to say it the same
way, no matter what you're

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:44.791
talking to you, but the way
you want to be responded to

00:43:44.791 --> 00:43:45.890
could change.

00:43:45.890 --> 00:43:48.320
The next one is definitely
design your architecture

00:43:48.320 --> 00:43:49.420
for every use case.

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:52.120
And from what you could
see in my previous slides,

00:43:52.120 --> 00:43:54.080
architecture can
be easily expanded

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:56.100
to add more and more clients.

00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:57.770
And that's something
we wanted to be

00:43:57.770 --> 00:44:00.160
able to do so that we weren't
stuck a couple of months

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:02.660
down the road when we had some
new channel we wanted to add,

00:44:02.660 --> 00:44:05.030
and then we had to completely
re-architect our solution

00:44:05.030 --> 00:44:06.570
in order to support it.

00:44:06.570 --> 00:44:08.820
And then the last point is
share code between teams.

00:44:08.820 --> 00:44:13.010
So if you look at our last
layer, the action layer,

00:44:13.010 --> 00:44:14.709
we actually export
all of our code.

00:44:14.709 --> 00:44:17.000
It's all on Google Cloud
Function, so it's all on node.

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:20.280
We export all of our code to
an internal NPM repository.

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:21.700
And then we can just have--

00:44:21.700 --> 00:44:23.600
different channels can
just import that code,

00:44:23.600 --> 00:44:25.910
add three lines of
code to their channel,

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:28.400
and then they can have
the exact same experience

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:30.000
in two different channels.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:31.560
So that's one big takeaway.

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:33.940
And that's going to save
everybody a whole lot of time,

00:44:33.940 --> 00:44:34.440
right?

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:35.854
So where are we going next?

00:44:35.854 --> 00:44:37.520
First of all, we want
to see what we can

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:38.540
do with Knowledge Connectors.

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:40.914
I had a little bit of time to
play around with it myself,

00:44:40.914 --> 00:44:44.179
and it seemed very, very easy
and very quick to set up,

00:44:44.179 --> 00:44:46.220
so we want to see what we
can do to productionize

00:44:46.220 --> 00:44:47.700
that in the future.

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:49.850
And we also want to look
into enabling new intents,

00:44:49.850 --> 00:44:52.190
like where's my order,
add to shopping list,

00:44:52.190 --> 00:44:54.172
or finding products
inside of a store.

00:44:54.172 --> 00:44:55.880
And we're also looking
into new channels,

00:44:55.880 --> 00:44:59.770
like the IVR system and,
also, the Google Home.

00:44:59.770 --> 00:45:03.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

